Falsifies `paging::aarch64::verification::a_block_keeps_address_and_permissions_apart`.

The defect: `Aarch64::block_entry` sets bits [1:0] to `0b11`, the way `leaf_entry` does.
At L3 that means PAGE, and the leaf encoder is correct to set it. At L1 and L2 the same
two bits mean TABLE, so the "block" is a table descriptor whose address field is the
start of somebody's 2 MiB of memory: the hardware walks that memory as a page table.
`is_block` then reads the entry as a table too, so the software walk agrees with the
hardware and translation fails rather than lying, which is why the round trip alone is
not enough and the bit pattern is asserted as a literal.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(block & 0b11, 0b01, "not a block descriptor")`.

diff --git a/crates/paging/src/aarch64.rs b/crates/paging/src/aarch64.rs
index 497feba6..9a543b50 100644
--- a/crates/paging/src/aarch64.rs
+++ b/crates/paging/src/aarch64.rs
@@ -173,7 +173,7 @@ impl PageFormat for Aarch64 {
         match size {
             PageSize::Size4KiB => Some(Self::leaf_entry(pa, flags)),
             PageSize::Size2MiB | PageSize::Size1GiB => {
-                Some((pa & ADDR_MASK & !(size.bytes() - 1)) | Self::attrs(flags) | VALID)
+                Some((pa & ADDR_MASK & !(size.bytes() - 1)) | Self::attrs(flags) | TABLE_OR_PAGE | VALID)
             }
         }
     }
